.TH GXWI-NETWORK-MANAGER 1 2026-10-19 "Peios gxwi-network-manager 0.1.0"
.SH NAME
gxwi-network-manager \- see and change this machine's networks, profiles, name resolution and firewall on a GXWI desktop
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B gxwi-network-manager
.SH DESCRIPTION
Network Manager is the desktop's window on this machine's networking and its firewall.
Open it from the launcher (type network).
It takes no arguments.
Down the side are three groups.
.PP
Network: Overview maps each interface to the network it is on and the profile it uses, with its addresses, routes, traffic and DHCP lease, and Renew lease and Reapply.
Networks names each network the machine has stood on and sets its trust level, Private or Public.
Profiles and Profile rules edit the profiles and which interface uses which.
DNS looks a name up and shows the way the question went, flushes resolvd's cache, and sets fallback servers, search domains and static names.
.PP
Firewall: Exposure shows which services can be reached from private networks, public networks and this machine, and changes a cell in one step.
Rules writes the firewall's rules, and Test a connection shows what the firewall would do with one.
Activity shows what the firewall has been deciding.
.PP
System: Port reservations says who can listen on a port.
Auditing sets the lowest level of audit event recorded.
Advanced shows the machine's DHCP identity, and who can look at and change netd's and resolvd's settings.
Permissions..., beside a reservation or beside netd's or resolvd's access, opens the permissions editor, which shows it read-only to anyone who cannot change it.
.PP
What it shows comes from netd, resolvd, the kernel and the registry under Machine\eSystem\eNetwork.
What it changes it writes to the registry, as the person, after checking it as the machine would; what the machine would refuse is not written, and the status line says why.
A change that would cut a desktop connection that is open now asks first.
Saving a rule, changing a cell on Exposure, and other changes that could cut the person off show a Keep this change? bar, and are undone after 30 seconds unless kept.
The countdown runs on the machine, not in the browser.
.PP
Firewall listener, connection, counter, and recent-audit panels display constructed example data.
Each affected section is labeled Example data, and the status line says Example.
The application judges those examples against real rules and machine context; they are not a live traffic inventory.
Saved rules and non-firewall network state are real.
Verify changes with the intended actual connection rather than the example panels.
.PP
What a person can change is what the registry lets them write, key by key, asked of the machine.
Where they cannot, a section is shown read-only and says once that changing it needs an administrator.
Exposure and Activity are for administrators only.
Renew lease and Reapply need netd's Network Control right, which Administrators hold.
It reads the machine again every two seconds, and its settings whenever the registry changes.
.SH CONFIGURATION
Network and firewall configuration is read from Machine\eSystem\eNetwork.
The netd, resolvd, and kernel registry manuals define the settings and their application timing.
.SH SECURITY
Registry writes, daemon control, and restricted views use their own access checks.
The interface does not infer all rights from group membership.
A guarded network change can revert after 30 seconds; other changes have no automatic rollback.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The application session ended normally.
.TP
.B 1
A connection, initialization, or application error prevented normal operation.
.TP
.B 64
Invalid command arguments.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B GXWI_DISPLAY
The display connection supplied by the desktop session.
Launch through the desktop so inherited session descriptors and identity are available.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /usr/share/apps/dev.peios.gxwi-network-manager.toml
Its application declaration.
.TP
.I /run/netd/control.sock
netd's control socket: its status, renewals, and reapplying.
.TP
.I /run/resolvd/resolv.sock
resolvd's socket: its status, lookups, and flushing its cache.
.TP
.I /usr/bin/gxwi-sd-editor
The permissions editor, which Permissions... opens.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR net (1),
.BR resolv (1),
.BR gxwi-sd-editor (1).
.PP
Network Manager, at learn.peios.org.
